mode: "100644"
text: "# The Response Playbook\n\nOnce triaged (`triage-and-severity.md`) and a human is in the loop, this is how to respond. The arc:\n**Acknowledge → Investigate → Respond → Follow Up.**\n\n> The agent **drafts**; a **human approves and posts.** For high-severity, legal/leadership approve the\n> wording first (`escalation-pause-and-safety.md`).\n\n## 1. Acknowledge — fast, human, honest\n\n- **Speed:** get a first acknowledgment out **within ~30–60 minutes** (up to ~2 hours) of identifying a\n  real issue — **even without all the answers.** Silence creates a vacuum that rumor and misinformation\n  fill, and the algorithm amplifies the negativity in the meantime.\n- **You don't need every answer yet:** *\"We're aware of this and we're looking into it now. We'll share\n  more shortly.\"*\n- **Speak human, drop the legalese.** Empathetic, plain, in brand voice (a touch more serious than\n  usual). *\"We understand the concern and we're sorry for the worry this caused.\"*\n- **Don't be defensive, don't spin, don't argue in the replies.**\n\n## 2. Investigate — get the facts (in parallel)\n\nWhile the acknowledgment holds the space, **confirm what happened** internally. Don't commit to a cause\nor a fix you haven't verified. Keep the human/decision-makers aligned on what's known.\n\n## 3. Respond — the substance\n\nTailor to the type (from triage):\n\n- **Your fault / a real failure:** **take responsibility plainly** (no non-apology \"we're sorry if\n  anyone was offended\"), say **what you're doing about it**, and **pair words with a real operational\n  action** — a fix, a refund, a policy change. Words without action read as PR.\n- **Misinformation / a false claim:** **correct the record with evidence**, calmly and factually — do\n  **not** apologize for something that didn't happen. Link proof.\n- **A deepfake / impersonation:** state clearly it's fake, show evidence, report it, and get the true\n  version in front of your audience fast.\n- **Centralize:** for anything sustained, use a **pinned post / landing page** with **timestamped\n  updates** so people have one source of truth instead of chasing fragments.\n\n## 4. Follow up — close the loop\n\nWhen resolved, **say what happened, what you did, and what changes.** A well-handled crisis can leave\ntrust *higher* than before. Then debrief internally: what triggered it, what to prevent, what to add to\nthe playbook.\n\n## Transparency over scrubbing\n\n**Don't delete legitimate criticism to make it look clean.** Scrubbing genuine complaints triggers the\n**Streisand effect** — hiding it draws far more attention and reads as a cover-up. (What you *may*\nremove is in `moderation.md`.) Transparency is the defensive move.\n\n## Pre-approved holding lines (draft, then human-approves)\n\n- Blunt founder voice: *\"We messed up the shipping this week. That's on us, not you. Here's what we're\n  doing about it, starting today: [action]. Updates as we have them.\"*\n- Warm studio voice: *\"We know some of you have been waiting longer than you should have — we're sorry,\n  and we hear you. Here's exactly what we're doing to fix it, and we'll keep you posted right here.\"*\n\nKeep critical wording **approved** before it goes out; never let the agent post a crisis response on its\nown.\n"
